## Citations (binding)

- An on-screen string is a REFERENCE, never a composition:
  `{"line": 7}` renders line L7 verbatim. You choose WHICH line carries each
  beat; the system renders its exact words. A string you typed yourself is
  wrong by construction — the resolver only accepts ids.
- Over-long lines: when the line you want exceeds its slot's character cap,
  set `"fit": "head"` (keep the leading segment) or `"fit": "tail"` (keep
  the trailing one) — the system trims at a word boundary. With `"fit"`
  omitted ("full") the line must fit the cap as-is, so cite a shorter line
  instead of forcing a long one.
- Accent: `"accent": "<substring>"` wraps one substring of the RESOLVED
  text in emphasis marks. The substring must exist in the resolved text —
  a trim that cuts your accent away is a citation error.
- ui-scene captions cite ONLY lines from the page that scene shows; every
  other scene cites any presented line.
- If your previous storyboard was rejected: a rejection names the slot and
  the cause (unknown line id, missed accent, caption from the wrong page,
  resolved text over its cap). Fix THE CITATION — never answer a rejection
  by writing strings.

## Narration (配音词, spoken voiceover)

Every scene MAY carry `"narration"`: the spoken voiceover line for that
scene — one or two natural sentences, ≤ 20 chars per second of the scene.
Narration is SPEECH, not on-screen copy: it never renders as text, and the
citation law does NOT apply to it — this is your creative channel: rephrase
the page's facts into natural spoken language. It must still be TRUE to the
manifest (never invent a claim, a number, or a feature the page does not
state), and it speaks the FILM's language: the pinned language above when
one is given; otherwise the PAGE's own language — the film presents the
product, so the product's voice leads, and the language of the user's ask /
instruction is NEVER a source. Omit the field (or null) for a scene that
plays better silent — a divider beat or a fast words cut often wants no
voiceover. Narrate the scenes where speech carries the story (hero, ui
walks, reveal, end).

## Output (JSON, no prose)

{
  "name": string | null,   // a short display name for this film; null if unsure
  "theme": {"canvas": "#RRGGBB", "ink": "#RRGGBB"} | null,  // null = default
  "language": string,      // the film's spoken language (ISO code, e.g. "en") — the
                           // pinned value when one is given above, else the page's own
                           // language. Every narration line is written in it.
  "scenes": [ ... ]        // 4..16 scenes, ordered
}

Scene types (discriminator `type`); every scene carries `seconds` (a duration
INTENT — the system assigns exact frames). `REF` below =
`{"line": int, "fit": "full"|"head"|"tail", "accent": string | null}`
(`fit`/`accent` optional):

- {"type":"words","seconds":1.5..4,"dark":bool,"lines":[REF]}  // 1..4 lines; each resolves to ≤30 chars
- {"type":"hero","seconds":2..5,"word":REF,"sub":REF}  // word resolves to ≤16 chars, sub ≤90
- {"type":"ui","seconds":3..10,"page":int,"shot":int,"frame":bool,"focuses":[{"at":s,"element":"s<shot>e<j>"}],"captions":[{"at":s,"text":REF}]}
  // page = a presented page index WITH a screenshot; shot = which captured
  // view of it (0 = the top of the page — pick a section shot when the
  // walk's story lives below the fold). frame: true = the page shown in a
  // browser-chrome card (the "this is a real page" beat); false = full-bleed,
  // the camera fills the canvas with the page itself (the immersive push).
  // A focus names an element id from that shot's camera-target list — the
  // system computes the move (never invent coordinates; the bare
  // {"at","x","y","zoom"} form exists only for shots that list no targets).
  // 1..3 focuses, `at` ascending, each < seconds; 0..3 captions, each
  // resolving to ≤60 chars, each ≥0.5s apart
- {"type":"divider","seconds":2..5,"label":REF,"roll":[REF]}  // label ≤40; 1..5 roll words, each ≤24
- {"type":"plate","seconds":3..7,"title":REF,"cards":[{"big":REF|null,"label":REF}],"backdrop":"aurora"|"dunes"|"mist"|"tide"}
  // the numbers beat: 2..4 glass cards over a calm backdrop. ONLY when the
  // page actually carries numbers or very short labels (a card's big/label
  // cites a line like any other slot — never invent a number). big ≤8
  // chars or null (a phrase card), label ≤24; title ≤40. At most 2 plates
  // in the whole film.
- {"type":"reveal","seconds":2..5,"lead":REF,"word":REF,"sub":REF}  // ≤60 / ≤24 / ≤90
- {"type":"end","seconds":2.5..6,"name":REF,"tagline":REF}  // ≤24 / ≤90

Every scene may add "narration": string | null (see Narration above).

Binding bounds: total Σ seconds 30..115; no duplicate string within one scene
(after resolution); no two scenes with identical content; no empty strings.

